{
  "extends": "../tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": false,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": false,
    /* One program over every package, so a declaration graph that crosses a package (@mistriwork/mistri/commands reaches
       the engine's types) is emitted whole. tsc writes each package's tree under the stage, and
       scripts/build-packages.mjs moves packages/<name>/src's output to packages/<name>/dist. */
    "outDir": "../node_modules/.cache/build-stage",
    "rootDir": "../packages",
    /* A build starts from an empty stage, so it emits everything: the checkout's incremental state says what was
       checked, not what is on disk here. */
    "incremental": false,
    "tsBuildInfoFile": null,
    /* The main CLI build omits declarations and source maps.
    
       Originally this package had one entry point, a `bin`. Nothing imported it,
       so the 33 `.d.ts` files described types no consumer can reach; and Node
       ignores a source map unless it is started with `--enable-source-maps`,
       which nothing here does. Together they were a third of the tarball —
       930kB of files whose only reader was `npm pack`.
    
       The cost, stated: a stack trace from a bug report now names a line in
       compiled JavaScript rather than in TypeScript. The build is
       reproducible from the tag, so that is a lookup rather than a loss, and
       turning either back on is one line. The server-only commands package
       entry now emits its required declaration graph separately through
       tsconfig.commands.json. */
    "declaration": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    /* The comments here say why, and since 0.9.9 they ship: whoever reads
       dist/ — an operator chasing a stack trace without source maps, an
       agent reading the installed package — reads the same reasons the
       repository does. They were dropped for size (about a third of the
       server's bytes); the owner chose readable over smaller. */
    "removeComments": false
    /* `npm run build` checks this program (scripts/build-packages.mjs): a build from a tag or a tarball has not run
       `npm test` first, and an emit that ignores a type error ships it. tsconfig.commands.json, the same sources again
       for their declarations, is emitted with --noCheck rather than checked twice. */
  },
  "include": ["../packages/*/src"],
  /* The client is Vite's, not tsc's: `vite build` bundles src/client into
     dist/client at the same `npm run build`. With `allowJs` on and `noEmit`
     off, tsc would otherwise *emit* the client too — a transpiled copy that
     the bundle then landed on top of. The plain `tsc --noEmit` check still
     sees these modules, because that runs from tsconfig.json. Each package's
     src/shared and src/adapters are compiled here as before; the client reaches
     them through the bundle. mistri's src/embed (@mistriwork/mistri/react) is the client
     built once more, by vite.embed.config.ts, and is Vite's for the same reason. */
  "exclude": [
    "../packages/*/src/client",
    "../packages/*/src/embed",
    /* @mistriwork/ui's tests sit beside its primitives (Vitest runs them); a package ships what they test, not them. */
    "../packages/*/src/**/*.test.ts",
    "../packages/*/src/**/*.test.tsx"
  ]
}
